1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:01,350
A flip book.

2
00:00:01,287 --> 00:00:02,831
A slide under a microscope.

3
00:00:02,831 --> 00:00:03,952
Two perfect copies.

4
00:00:04,352 --> 00:00:08,440
In the college lab, Diya looked
at a slide of an onion root tip.

5
00:00:08,440 --> 00:00:10,787
Some cells had a plain round nucleus.

6
00:00:10,787 --> 00:00:14,662
Others had dark threads, lined
up, or pulled into two groups.

7
00:00:15,111 --> 00:00:19,332
She wants to be a doctor, and she asked:
are these different kinds of cells,

8
00:00:19,332 --> 00:00:21,606
or one thing caught at different moments?

9
00:00:21,956 --> 00:00:23,957
They are snapshots of one process.

10
00:00:23,957 --> 00:00:26,799
The cell is sharing its
copied chromosomes equally.

11
00:00:26,799 --> 00:00:28,134
It is called mitosis.

12
00:00:28,634 --> 00:00:33,154
By the end, you will name the four
stages of mitosis, in order, and spot

13
00:00:33,154 --> 00:00:34,599
each one on a slide.

14
00:00:35,249 --> 00:00:36,988
First, what is mitosis?

15
00:00:37,338 --> 00:00:41,855
Mitosis is the division of the nucleus,
so that each daughter cell gets

16
00:00:41,855 --> 00:00:45,011
exactly the same chromosomes
as the parent cell.

17
00:00:45,461 --> 00:00:49,510
Because the number stays the same,
it is called equational division.

18
00:00:49,510 --> 00:00:53,054
A parent with two n chromosomes
makes daughters with two n.

19
00:00:53,504 --> 00:00:55,169
The nucleus divides first.

20
00:00:55,169 --> 00:00:57,876
That is karyokinesis,
karyo meaning nucleus.

21
00:00:57,876 --> 00:00:59,587
Then the cytoplasm divides.

22
00:00:59,587 --> 00:01:00,917
That is cytokinesis.

23
00:01:01,567 --> 00:01:05,678
Let's zoom in, all the way to
one chromosome ready for mitosis.

24
00:01:06,028 --> 00:01:09,693
Here is the onion root tip,
where cells divide fast.

25
00:01:10,193 --> 00:01:12,135
Zoom into one dividing cell.

26
00:01:12,135 --> 00:01:14,954
Its chromosomes are thick and easy to see.

27
00:01:15,454 --> 00:01:16,978
Zoom into one chromosome.

28
00:01:16,978 --> 00:01:20,771
It is made of two sister chromatids,
two copies made in S phase.

29
00:01:21,271 --> 00:01:23,610
They are held together at the centromere.

30
00:01:23,610 --> 00:01:26,431
On its surface sit small
discs called kinetochores.

31
00:01:26,431 --> 00:01:28,108
Spindle fibres hook on here.

32
00:01:28,758 --> 00:01:31,522
Now let's watch one
cell go through mitosis.

33
00:01:31,872 --> 00:01:32,772
Stage one.

34
00:01:32,697 --> 00:01:33,597
Prophase.

35
00:01:33,418 --> 00:01:37,119
The chromosome threads start
to condense, to coil up thick.

36
00:01:37,119 --> 00:01:41,645
The centrosomes move to opposite sides,
and send out fibres called asters.

37
00:01:42,395 --> 00:01:47,480
By the end of prophase, the Golgi
complex, the ER, the nucleolus and the

38
00:01:47,480 --> 00:01:50,199
nuclear envelope can no longer be seen.

39
00:01:50,949 --> 00:01:51,849
Stage two.

40
00:01:51,798 --> 00:01:52,698
Metaphase.

41
00:01:52,589 --> 00:01:54,892
The nuclear envelope is fully gone.

42
00:01:54,892 --> 00:01:59,099
Spindle fibres attach to the
kinetochores, and the chromosomes line up

43
00:01:59,099 --> 00:02:00,397
across the middle.

44
00:02:00,397 --> 00:02:02,603
This line is the metaphase plate.

45
00:02:03,353 --> 00:02:04,253
Stage three.

46
00:02:04,190 --> 00:02:05,090
Anaphase.

47
00:02:04,846 --> 00:02:08,737
The centromeres split, and the sister
chromatids move to opposite poles,

48
00:02:08,737 --> 00:02:10,980
centromere first, arms trailing behind.

49
00:02:11,730 --> 00:02:12,630
Stage four.

50
00:02:12,622 --> 00:02:13,522
Telophase.

51
00:02:13,407 --> 00:02:16,139
The chromosomes reach
the poles and uncoil.

52
00:02:16,139 --> 00:02:20,567
A nuclear envelope forms around each
group, and the nucleolus, Golgi and

53
00:02:20,567 --> 00:02:21,720
ER come back.

54
00:02:22,470 --> 00:02:25,375
Each stage has one clue
you can spot on a slide.

55
00:02:25,725 --> 00:02:30,016
In prophase, the threads are coiling
up, and the nucleus is still there.

56
00:02:30,516 --> 00:02:34,805
In metaphase, the chromosomes are
at their thickest, all in one line.

57
00:02:34,805 --> 00:02:37,582
This is the best stage
to study their shape.

58
00:02:38,082 --> 00:02:40,952
In anaphase, you see
two groups moving apart.

59
00:02:40,952 --> 00:02:43,161
Each chromatid is shaped like a V.

60
00:02:43,661 --> 00:02:48,448
In telophase, you see two groups at the
two ends, with a new nuclear envelope

61
00:02:48,448 --> 00:02:49,817
forming around each.

62
00:02:50,467 --> 00:02:53,043
Side by side, prophase and telophase.

63
00:02:53,043 --> 00:02:56,504
They are like a film played
forward, then backward.

64
00:02:56,504 --> 00:02:57,419
Chromosomes.

65
00:02:57,419 --> 00:02:59,220
In prophase they condense.

66
00:02:59,220 --> 00:03:01,333
In telophase they uncoil again.

67
00:03:02,083 --> 00:03:03,348
Nuclear envelope.

68
00:03:03,348 --> 00:03:05,317
In prophase it breaks down.

69
00:03:05,317 --> 00:03:08,726
In telophase a new one
forms, around each group.

70
00:03:09,476 --> 00:03:11,274
Nucleolus, Golgi and ER.

71
00:03:11,274 --> 00:03:14,576
They vanish in prophase,
and come back in telophase.

72
00:03:15,326 --> 00:03:16,226
Your turn.

73
00:03:16,164 --> 00:03:20,586
On a slide, you see thick chromosomes
in one neat line across the middle

74
00:03:20,586 --> 00:03:21,578
of the cell.

75
00:03:21,578 --> 00:03:22,917
Which stage is it?

76
00:03:22,917 --> 00:03:24,150
Pause and decide.

77
00:03:27,350 --> 00:03:28,250
Metaphase!

78
00:03:28,069 --> 00:03:30,803
The chromosomes line up
at the metaphase plate.

79
00:03:31,453 --> 00:03:32,518
Remember this.

80
00:03:32,518 --> 00:03:33,418
PMAT.

81
00:03:33,403 --> 00:03:36,229
Prophase, metaphase, anaphase, telophase.

82
00:03:36,829 --> 00:03:38,252
Now, a worked example.

83
00:03:38,252 --> 00:03:39,152
Six events.

84
00:03:39,027 --> 00:03:41,225
Before the chromatids split, or after?

85
00:03:41,575 --> 00:03:43,514
Chromatin starts to condense.

86
00:03:43,514 --> 00:03:45,043
Before, in prophase.

87
00:03:45,493 --> 00:03:47,596
Chromatids move to opposite poles.

88
00:03:47,596 --> 00:03:48,980
After, in anaphase.

89
00:03:49,430 --> 00:03:52,155
Chromosomes line up at
the metaphase plate.

90
00:03:52,155 --> 00:03:53,681
Before, in metaphase.

91
00:03:54,131 --> 00:03:56,972
A nuclear envelope
forms around each group.

92
00:03:56,972 --> 00:03:58,513
After, in telophase.

93
00:03:58,963 --> 00:04:01,172
The centrosomes move to opposite poles.

94
00:04:01,172 --> 00:04:02,493
Before, in prophase.

95
00:04:02,943 --> 00:04:04,812
And the nucleolus comes back.

96
00:04:04,812 --> 00:04:06,242
After, in telophase.

97
00:04:06,892 --> 00:04:08,354
Now, the exam trap.

98
00:04:08,704 --> 00:04:12,926
Many students think chromosomes are
best studied in prophase, because that

99
00:04:12,926 --> 00:04:15,089
is when they first become visible.

100
00:04:15,489 --> 00:04:17,784
But they are still condensing then.

101
00:04:17,784 --> 00:04:20,574
They are thickest and
clearest in metaphase.

102
00:04:20,574 --> 00:04:23,968
Metaphase is the best stage
to study chromosome shape.

103
00:04:24,618 --> 00:04:26,111
Where is this asked?

104
00:04:26,111 --> 00:04:30,910
As the order of the stages, the event
that marks each stage, and labelling

105
00:04:30,910 --> 00:04:31,810
a diagram.

106
00:04:32,381 --> 00:04:33,558
Exam level now.

107
00:04:33,558 --> 00:04:34,884
Read the question.

108
00:04:34,884 --> 00:04:36,111
Pause and solve.

109
00:04:41,511 --> 00:04:42,848
The answer is C.

110
00:04:43,348 --> 00:04:47,702
Metaphase begins when the nuclear
envelope has completely broken down.

111
00:04:48,202 --> 00:04:52,647
Centromeres split in anaphase, condensing
starts in prophase, and the nucleolus

112
00:04:52,647 --> 00:04:54,193
comes back in telophase.

113
00:04:54,793 --> 00:04:56,333
Here is your recap.

114
00:04:56,333 --> 00:04:57,915
Screenshot this card.

115
00:05:01,115 --> 00:05:03,301
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

116
00:05:03,701 --> 00:05:04,601
One.

117
00:05:04,274 --> 00:05:07,566
Name the four stages of mitosis, in order.

118
00:05:08,466 --> 00:05:09,366
Two.

119
00:05:08,712 --> 00:05:09,612
What are kinetochores?

120
00:05:10,351 --> 00:05:11,251
Three.

121
00:05:10,908 --> 00:05:13,382
Why is mitosis called equational division?

122
00:05:14,482 --> 00:05:17,438
Next time: a plant cell has a stiff wall.

123
00:05:17,438 --> 00:05:19,520
So how does it split in two?

124
00:05:19,520 --> 00:05:23,341
We find out in cytokinesis and
the significance of mitosis.
